---
import ProsePage from "../components/ProsePage.astro";
import { CONSENT_GRANTED, CONSENT_KEY } from "../consent/consent-store.ts";
import { PROVIDER_CHOICE_KEY } from "../scripts/provider-choice.ts";
import { PRIVACY_PAGE } from "../site/footer-pages.ts";
import { PRIVACY_CHANGED_ON } from "../site/privacy-changed-on.ts";
import {
  contactAddress,
  PRIVACY_CONTACT_EMAIL,
  SITE_HOST,
  SITE_MAINTAINER,
} from "../site/site-owner.ts";
import { GITHUB, GOOGLE_MAPS, MAP_PROVIDERS_SECTION, OPENFREEMAP } from "../site/third-parties.ts";

const contact = contactAddress(PRIVACY_CONTACT_EMAIL);
---

<ProsePage
  title={PRIVACY_PAGE.label}
  description="What maplacejs's site sends where, what it keeps in your browser, and your rights."
>
  <Fragment slot="lede">
    What this site sends where, what it keeps in your browser, and your rights.
  </Fragment>

  <section aria-labelledby="in-your-browser">
    <h2 id="in-your-browser">In your browser</h2>
    <p>
      This site's pages set no cookies of their own. Once you choose to load the maps, the site
      keeps one entry in your browser's local storage, <code>{CONSENT_KEY}</code> set to{" "}
      <code>{CONSENT_GRANTED}</code>, so later visits show the maps without asking again. Turning
      the maps off removes it. If your browser keeps no site data, your choice lasts for the page
      you made it on.
    </p>
    <p>
      Choosing Google or MapLibre for the examples keeps one more entry, in session storage,{" "}
      <code>{PROVIDER_CHOICE_KEY}</code>, so the examples open on it again while the tab stays
      open; the browser drops it when the session ends.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby={MAP_PROVIDERS_SECTION}>
    <h2 id={MAP_PROVIDERS_SECTION}>The maps</h2>
    <p>
      The maps on this site come from two services:{" "}
      <a href={`#${GOOGLE_MAPS.id}`}>{GOOGLE_MAPS.name}</a> draws the examples you run on Google,
      and <a href={`#${OPENFREEMAP.id}`}>{OPENFREEMAP.name}</a> the map under the examples you run
      on MapLibre. Every map waits behind a placeholder, and nothing is asked of either until you
      choose “Load maps” on one of them or turn the maps on in the footer. That one choice covers
      both: from then on, each map loads as it scrolls into view, on this visit and later ones,
      until you turn the maps off. Pages without a map load neither.
    </p>
    <p>
      Loading the maps rests on your consent (Article 6(1)(a) of the GDPR). You can withdraw it at
      any time with the maps switch in the footer of every page: it forgets your choice and reloads
      the page, since a loaded map can't be removed from an open page. Withdrawing doesn't affect
      what the maps loaded before.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby={GOOGLE_MAPS.id}>
    <h2 id={GOOGLE_MAPS.id}>{GOOGLE_MAPS.name}</h2>
    <p>
      Once a map on Google loads, your browser fetches Google's Maps JavaScript API, then the
      map's tiles, images and fonts as you look around. With each request Google receives your IP
      address, what your browser says about itself (its user agent), the address of the page the
      map is on, and which part of the map you're viewing. The examples with a route also ask
      Google's Routes service for directions between their own stops. The site never asks for
      your location.
    </p>
    <p>
      Google is a US company, certified under the EU-U.S. Data Privacy Framework. It may use its
      own cookies under its terms, and its Privacy Policy says what it does with what it receives:
    </p>
    <ul>
      {GOOGLE_MAPS.terms.map(({ label, url }) => <li><a href={url}>{label}</a></li>)}
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby={OPENFREEMAP.id}>
    <h2 id={OPENFREEMAP.id}>{OPENFREEMAP.name}</h2>
    <p>
      Once a map on MapLibre loads, your browser fetches its style, tiles, fonts and icons from
      OpenFreeMap, at {OPENFREEMAP.domains.map((domain) => <code>{domain}</code>)}. With each
      request OpenFreeMap receives your IP address, what your browser says about itself (its user
      agent), the address of the page the map is on, and which part of the map you're viewing.
      OpenFreeMap may serve them through Cloudflare's network, which then receives the same
      requests under its own privacy policy (linked under <a href="#host">Who serves this site</a>).
      OpenFreeMap sets no cookies, and its Privacy Policy says what it keeps and for how long:
    </p>
    <ul>
      {OPENFREEMAP.terms.map(({ label, url }) => <li><a href={url}>{label}</a></li>)}
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby={GITHUB.id}>
    <h2 id={GITHUB.id}>{GITHUB.name}</h2>
    <p>
      The star button on the home and example pages shows how many people starred maplacejs on
      GitHub. To show it, your browser asks GitHub's API for the repository's count when the page
      opens, without cookies; with that request GitHub receives your IP address, your browser's
      user agent and this site's address. The site asks for the count on the basis of its
      legitimate interest in showing the project's star count (Article 6(1)(f) of the GDPR).
      GitHub is a US company, certified under the EU-U.S. Data Privacy Framework. Its Privacy
      Statement says what it does with what it receives:
    </p>
    <ul>
      {GITHUB.terms.map(({ label, url }) => <li><a href={url}>{label}</a></li>)}
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="host">
    <h2 id="host">Who serves this site</h2>
    <p>
      {SITE_HOST.name} serves this site's pages. As with any website, it receives each request
      your browser makes here: your IP address, your browser's user agent and the page asked for.{" "}
      {SITE_HOST.name} also keeps statistics of those requests for the site, so its maintainer can
      see which pages are read. Both rest on legitimate interest (Article 6(1)(f) of the GDPR): in
      delivering the site and keeping it safe from abuse, and in knowing which pages are read.{" "}
      {SITE_HOST.name} keeps the requests for as long as its privacy policy sets, and the
      statistics for the period its analytics keep them on the site's plan, at least 31 days.{" "}
      {SITE_HOST.name} is a US company, certified under the EU-U.S. Data Privacy Framework. To tell
      people from automated traffic, it may set a short-lived security cookie,{" "}
      <code>{SITE_HOST.securityCookie}</code>, which {SITE_HOST.name} keeps, not this site. Its{" "}
      <a href={SITE_HOST.privacyPolicy}>privacy policy</a> says what it does with what it receives.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="contact">
    <h2 id="contact">Who runs it</h2>
    <p>
      maplacejs and this site are maintained by {SITE_MAINTAINER}, who is responsible for what
      this page describes (its controller). Questions about this page, or about your data, go to{" "}
      <a href={`mailto:${contact}`}>{contact}</a>. If you write, your message is kept as long as
      answering it needs, on the basis of legitimate interest in answering (Article 6(1)(f) of the
      GDPR).
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="your-rights">
    <h2 id="your-rights">Your rights</h2>
    <p>
      The site keeps no personal data of its own. The only personal data handled for it are what{" "}
      {SITE_HOST.name} receives with each request (your IP address, your browser's user agent and
      the page asked for) and any email you send. For those, you can ask for access, correction or
      deletion, for their use to be restricted, or for a copy, and you can object to what rests on
      legitimate interest: write to the address above. What {GOOGLE_MAPS.name},{" "}
      {OPENFREEMAP.name} and {GITHUB.name} do with what they receive is under their own policies,
      linked above. For what this site has your browser send them, you can also exercise your
      rights with the site.
    </p>
    <p>
      No law or contract requires you to give anything. What {SITE_HOST.name} receives with each
      request is needed for any page to load; without your consent the maps don't load, and nothing else
      changes.
    </p>
    <p>
      You can also complain to a data protection authority: in the EU, the one where you live or
      work; in the UK, the Information Commissioner's Office.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="changes">
    <h2 id="changes">Changes</h2>
    <p>Last changed: <time datetime={PRIVACY_CHANGED_ON}>{PRIVACY_CHANGED_ON}</time>.</p>
  </section>
</ProsePage>
